import BlueprintPage from '../components/BlueprintPage.astro'; const sections=[
{title:'Complex symptoms need context',paragraphs:['Changes in mood, sleep, energy, perception, thinking, behaviour, or functioning require individual assessment of history, health, treatment, and safety. A webpage cannot determine whether someone has bipolar disorder, schizophrenia or another condition. Similar experiences can have different causes, and people with the same diagnosis may have different needs. If a concern is affecting work, relationships, sleep or daily responsibilities, a clinical conversation can help clarify the next step.','Before an appointment, note when the change began, how it has varied over time and what others have observed. Include periods of unusually high or irritable energy, changes in sleep, concentration, impulsive decisions, withdrawal, unusual perceptions or beliefs, and any impact on safety. These are examples to discuss, not a checklist for self-diagnosis. Bring a current medicine list and earlier records if available; do not delay asking for help because a document is missing.']},
{title:'What may be considered during an assessment',paragraphs:['A clinician may ask about mood and energy over time, perception and thinking, physical health, sleep, medicines, substance use, family history, previous care and everyday functioning. If appropriate, they may seek additional information or arrange further assessment. The clinician should explain what remains uncertain, which alternatives are being considered and what information would help. Family members can offer observations with the person’s consent, while the patient’s privacy and participation remain important.','A diagnosis is not established by one symptom, online quiz or single conversation. Assessment considers context, duration, change from usual functioning, health factors and safety. It can identify strengths, support needs and questions that require follow-up. Read more about <a href="/assessments">assessment options</a> and <a href="/psychiatry">psychiatry consultations</a>.']},
{title:'Treatment and follow-up are individual',paragraphs:['A care plan may involve follow-up, medication discussion, psychological support, practical routines, additional assessment or referral. The options depend on the person and the evaluation. A prescribed medicine should be discussed with the clinician, including its purpose, possible benefits and risks, monitoring and review timing. Do not stop or change treatment based on website information alone.','Some people benefit from support from more than one professional. Therapy may help with coping, relationships, routines and understanding experiences alongside medical care. See <a href="/therapy">clinical psychology and therapy</a> and the <a href="/psychiatrist-vs-psychologist">guide to psychiatrist and psychologist roles</a>. Ask how clinicians coordinate care and what consent is needed to share information.']},
{title:'Urgent safety and how to request care',paragraphs:['If someone cannot stay safe, may harm themselves or another person, is severely confused, or has an urgent medical need, call 112 or go to the nearest emergency department. Mind Grace is not an emergency service. Do not wait for a routine appointment or an online reply when immediate safety is at risk. See <a href="/emergency">emergency resources</a>.','For routine care, review <a href="/fees">current fees</a>, <a href="/process">first-visit preparation</a> and <a href="/location">directions to the clinic</a>. Use <a href="/book">the appointment request route</a> or contact the clinic with a general question. A request is not confirmed until a date and time are agreed. Share only the basic information needed to coordinate; do not send a detailed medical history through a general enquiry.']}
];
